# E_lab(MeV) angle sigma(b) err ; Cassagnou et al. NP 33 (1962) 449, EXFOR A1475005 (SIG, Fig. 7, normalized to Freeman et al. NP 5 (1958) 148 at 1.47 MeV); x1/2 (both alphas counted), as for Mani 1964 and Jeronymo 1962 (EXFOR codes all three the same way, 3-LI-7(P,A)2-HE-4,,SIG); 5 % assigned per point; free norm, no prior. Checked Oct 2026: the fitted norm of ~1.87 is not a double count of the x1/2. Neither reading agrees with independent data: at 1.50 MeV the raw value (16.3 mb) is 1.4-1.5x and the halved one 0.70-0.76x the per-reaction sigma of Taskaev et al. NIM B 525 (2022) 55 (21.4 mb, alpha production, /2; their 0.60 MeV point is 1.84x Rolfs 1986), and the raw EXFOR values of Cassagnou are 1.51x below Mani's at 3.0 MeV (86.6 vs 130.7 mb), the ratio of the two fitted norms (1.87/1.26). Its scale is a ~1.5 discrepancy between the old data sets, absorbed by the free norm; do not use it as a normalisation anchor
